# *******************************************************************************
# S-CORE Architecture: Hermetic OpenSSL FFI Synchronization Patch for openssl-sys
# *******************************************************************************
#
# Context & Rationale for Reviewers:
# ----------------------------------
# The upstream crates.io `openssl-sys` build script (`build/main.rs`) is designed
# for standard Cargo workflows and violates Bazel automotive requirements in two
# critical ways:
#
# 1. Non-Hermetic Host Probing:
#    Upstream scans the build host's filesystem (`/usr/include`, `/usr/lib`) and
#    runs host `pkg-config`. In sandboxed Bazel builds, this introduces host
#    leakage, non-deterministic builds, and toolchain conflicts.
#
# 2. API Capabilities Contract (The Version Ceiling):
#    S-CORE compiles pristine OpenSSL 3.6.1 from source (`@score_openssl`). However,
#    this release of `openssl-sys` (0.9.117) only defines FFI bindings up to OpenSSL
#    3.5.0 (`ossl350`). Exporting "3.6.1" via `DEP_OPENSSL_VERSION_NUMBER` could cause
#    downstream crates to invoke 3.6 APIs missing from this crate. Capping the exported
#    version ensures the crate never advertises APIs beyond its physical bindings.
#
# Technical Solution Implemented in this Patch:
# ---------------------------------------------
# 1. Sandbox-Safe Build Info Ingestion (Steps 1 & 2):
#    Reads `openssl_build_info.txt` generated hermetically by `@score_openssl` via
#    the `OPENSSL_BUILD_INFO_PATH` environment variable set in `crate.annotation`.
#    Extracts the compiled OpenSSL version string and disabled `OPENSSL_NO_*` flags.
#
# 2. Fail-Fast Major Version Safety Lock (Steps 3 & 4):
#    OpenSSL introduces breaking ABI changes between major releases (e.g. 3.x -> 4.x).
#    The script scans `build/cfgs.rs` to detect the maximum supported major version.
#    If C++ requests a major version exceeding crate support, it panics immediately
#    at compile time to prevent memory corruption or runtime segmentation faults.
#
# 3. Dynamic Compatibility Ceiling (Steps 5 & 6):
#    OpenSSL 3.x guarantees strict binary and ABI backwards-compatibility across
#    all 3.x minor releases (e.g. binaries built for 3.5 run safely against 3.6.1).
#    The script scans `build/cfgs.rs` dynamically to discover the highest minor version
#    defined in the crate (e.g. minor 5 for `ossl350`), and caps the minor version
#    to that ceiling. If capped, patch level is reset to 0. This guarantees a safe FFI
#    capabilities boundary across crate versions with zero hardcoded version lists.
#
# 4. Bitwise Hex Version Calculation (Step 7):
#    Computes OpenSSL's official 32-bit hex version number using the standard
#    `0xMNNFF00f` encoding format:
#      - M   (Major, bits 31..28)
#      - NN  (Minor, bits 27..20)
#      - FF  (Fix/Patch, bits 19..12)
#      - 00f (Status, 'f' representing public release)
#
# 5. Delegated Configuration Flag Generation (Steps 8 & 9):
#    Emits `cargo:rustc-cfg=openssl` so rustc recognizes authentic OpenSSL and
#    activates `#[cfg(openssl)]` declarations throughout the crate.
#    Delegates to the crate's internal `cfgs::get()` function using the calculated
#    version hex, generating the complete set of version cfg flags (`ossl101`..`ossl350`).
#
# 6. Disabled Algorithm Gating (Step 11 - Issue #200):
#    Parses active `OPENSSL_NO_*` flags from C++ compilation and emits matching
#    `cargo:rustc-cfg=osslconf="OPENSSL_NO_*"` compiler flags. This ensures Rust
#    does not expose FFI declarations for algorithms disabled in C++ (e.g. SM4, OCSP),
#    preventing undefined symbol linker errors during LLVM coverage builds.
#
# 7. Verification & Downstream Acceptance:
#    - Builds cleanly in `score-crates` across both `//:openssl` and `//:openssl_sys`.
#    - Validated end-to-end in `inc_security_crypto`, where all cryptographic unit
#      tests, daemon IPC, and Rust `cryptoki` provider tests pass against these bindings.
# *******************************************************************************
